  <h1>India’s Cybersecurity Landscape — Threats and Policy Responses</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>As India’s digital economy expands rapidly — with UPI handling 120 billion+ transactions in 2023 — the risks of cyberattacks, data breaches, and digital frauds have also intensified. In 2024, The Hindu analysed India’s cybersecurity vulnerabilities, especially amid rising geopolitical cyber threats. For UPSC, cybersecurity links technology, security, and governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Cyber Incidents Reported (CERT-In, 2023): ~13 lakh cases.<br/>•	Sectors Affected: Banking, power, telecom, health, and government portals.<br/>•	Ransomware Attacks: Increasing frequency on hospitals and enterprises.<br/>•	India’s Rank: Among top 5 most cyber-attacked countries (Check Point, 2024).<br/>•	Digital Economy Size: ~$1 trillion by 2030 projection.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Threats<br/>1.	State-Sponsored Attacks: Targeting critical infrastructure (power grids, defence).<br/>2.	Financial Fraud: Phishing, UPI fraud, crypto scams.<br/>3.	Data Breaches: Health and Aadhaar-related leaks.<br/>4.	Ransomware: Locking systems until ransom paid.<br/>5.	Deepfakes & AI Misuse: Threats to elections, misinformation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	National Cyber Security Policy (2013): Outdated, revision pending.<br/>•	CERT-In (2004): Monitoring and response to cyber incidents.<br/>•	Personal Data Protection Act (2023): Privacy framework.<br/>•	National Critical Information Infrastructure Protection Centre (NCIIPC): Securing critical infra.<br/>•	Cyber Surakshit Bharat Initiative: Awareness and training.<br/>•	International Cooperation: Engagement with Quad, UN on cyber norms.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	UPI Security Protocols: Real-time fraud monitoring.<br/>•	Cybercrime Portals: Citizen reporting systems.<br/>•	Cyber Drills: CERT-In conducts simulations with banks, power sector.<br/>•	Capacity Building: New cyber forensics labs.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Policy Vacuum: New cybersecurity policy delayed.<br/>2.	Low Awareness: Citizens still vulnerable to phishing/fraud.<br/>3.	Skill Shortages: Need for 1 million+ cyber professionals.<br/>4.	Tech Dependence: Reliance on foreign hardware/software.<br/>5.	Jurisdictional Issues: Global nature of cybercrime complicates enforcement.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	National Security: Cyber warfare is the new battleground.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Secure digital ecosystem vital for $5T economy goal.<br/>•	Democracy: Election integrity at risk from misinformation/deepfakes.<br/>•	Social Trust: Citizens’ confidence in digital services depends on safety.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Science & Tech, Security): Cybersecurity, IT Act, CERT-In.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Digital governance, rights vs security.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Cybersecurity is national security.”<br/>o	“Digital trust is the foundation of a digital economy.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Technology and state security.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Update Cyber Policy: Comprehensive framework with clear responsibilities.<br/>2.	Build Capacity: Train cyber professionals, strengthen CERT-In.<br/>3.	Critical Infra Protection: Mandatory audits for energy, defence, health.<br/>4.	Public Awareness: Cyber hygiene campaigns at scale.<br/>5.	International Cooperation: Global treaties on cybercrime, joint drills.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s cybersecurity challenge is a moving target in an era of rapid digitalisation. A robust legal framework, skilled workforce, and international coordination are essential to safeguard its digital future. For UPSC aspirants, this theme illustrates the intersection of security, governance, and technology.</p>
